 1 big thing: Huang pitches a comeback 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photo: David Paul Morris/Bloomberg via Getty Images Ousted Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang says he'd be "honored" to return to the company he co-founded, Axios' Herb Scribner reports. 

 Huang, a towering figure in today's AI boom, left the hugely influential company on Nov. 12 amid reports of multiple affairs, including one with a woman whom the U.S. believes to be a Chinese spy. The fallout has sent tech stocks reeling and raised big questions about Nvidia's business practices, Commerce Secretary Howard Lutnick's handling of the company , and the state of the AI industry . 🤳 Huang wrote on X : "Since my departure, I've seen an outpouring of calls from Nvidia's employees, investors, business partners, and customers for me to return as CEO."

 "If the Board of Directors decides that my return is in the best interest of our shareholders, our employees, the company, and America, I would be honored to answer these calls and return as CEO." Some Nvidia employees and investors, AI boosters, and even President Trump have called for Huang's return.

 Trump said yesterday before leaving for his Asia trip: "I would not mind that." "I think he learned a big lesson — I guess maybe one lesson is more important than another." ✅ Reality check: Nvidia's board has shown zero interest in bringing Huang back.

 He and Nvidia general counsel Tim Teter are set to testify before Congress early next month, lawmakers announced today . Data: FactSet; Chart: Matt Phillips/Axios What's next: Nvidia's post-Huang era begins in earnest with the release of its third-quarter results after trading ends today, Axios' Matt Phillips writes . 

 Investors and analysts will read tea leaves and listen for hints about near-term changes the company is making (or expects the government to make) in response to Huang's resignation and the circumstances surrounding it. Go deeper . 

 
 
