 4. 🖼️ 1 for the road: NatGeo pics of the year 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Cover: Tony Wu/ National Geographic National Geographic's Pictures of the Year 2026 , out today, features 25 "extraordinary photographs revealing the moments, discoveries, and untold stories that defined the past year."

 The iconic yellow-bordered mag says: "Selected from more than one million images captured by National Geographic photographers around the globe, this year's collection offers a striking look at the natural world, human ingenuity and the remarkable ways people and wildlife navigate a changing planet." Above: " How, exactly, eels reproduce is a mystery that has long confounded biologists, and no human on record had ever photographed the act in the wild until Tony Wu did" in Kagoshima Prefecture, Japan, National Geographic says. 

 The cover photo shows a "pair of Abbott's moray eels (Gymnothorax eurostus) reaching the pinnacle of their spawning run, three tenths of a second after leaving the cover of coral. The male on the left is just releasing the jaw of [the] female." Photo: Jasper Doest/National Geographic 📸 Above, Lorena Masei playfully offers her hat to a horse at the Masei family farm in the Italian Alps.

 See the photos . 
 
